<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Félix Éboué</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Félix Éboué was a French colonial administrator born in French Guiana, the first Black governor of a French colony. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> he declared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>'s Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in August 1940, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> followed. Appointed governor-general, he promoted policies for African elites that influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville-conference">Brazzaville Conference</a> of 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felix-eboue.jpg" alt="Félix Éboué" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (Keystone-France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%A9lix_%C3%89bou%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adolphe Sylvestre Félix Éboué was born on 26 December 1884 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayenne">Cayenne</a>, French Guiana, the grandson of slaves and the fourth of five brothers. His father, Yves Urbain Éboué, was a gold prospector, and his mother, Marie Josephine Aurélie Léveillé, a shop owner born in Roura, raised her sons in the Guiana Créole tradition. A scholarship took him to secondary school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, where he captained the school football team on trips to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 1908 he graduated in administrative law from the École nationale de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> d'Outre-Mer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Éboué served for twenty years as a colonial administrator in Oubangui-Chari, and then in Martinique. A Socialist and a Freemason, he was made acting governor of Guadeloupe in 1936 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> government, the first man of black African descent appointed to so senior a post anywhere in the French colonies. Soon in conflict with the local elite, he left after two years and arrived in Fort Lamy as Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> on 4 January 1939. His acceptance of de Gaulle's authority in August 1940 gave the Free French movement a territorial base from which to exercise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and organise military forces.</p><p>As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> from 1940 to 1944 he published The New Indigenous Policy for French Equatorial Africa, which called for respect for African traditions, support for traditional leaders, the development of existing social structures and better working conditions. It served as a basis for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville-conference">Brazzaville Conference</a> of French colonial governors in 1944. He classed 200 educated Africans as notable évolués and reduced their taxes, and he placed some Gabonese civil servants in positions of authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Éboué married Eugénie Tell, and in 1946 their daughter Ginette married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>, the poet and later president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. He died of a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 17 May 1944, and in 1949 his remains were reburied in the Panthéon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, which made him the first black French man so honoured. He had received the Cross of the Liberation in 1941. A square in Paris, Place Félix-Éboué, and the Métro station Daumesnil Félix-Éboué bear his name, and in 2012 the main airport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayenne">Cayenne</a> was renamed in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Félix Éboué” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1940 Governor <b>Félix Éboué</b> declared for Free France, and Leclerc's forces struck north from Chad into Libya.</blockquote><a href="/state/chad-french-colony">Chad (French colony)</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain recognised him as leader of the Free French, and in August 1940 Chad, under its governor <b>Félix Éboué</b>, joined his cause, followed by most of French Equatorial Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-france">Free France</a></li><li><blockquote>In August 1940 <b>Félix Éboué</b>, governor of Chad and later governor-general, led the federation to join Free France, and the 1944 Brazzaville Conference promised reforms.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayenne">Cayenne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.94,-52.34&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Cayenne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Félix Éboué"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5651 4578h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5651 4578h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.94,-52.34&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
